+/* THE TWO EXITS, and they are one keyframe pair on one rung (`--motion-whisper`, 150ms —
+   §13's shortest). A line leaves this column exactly two ways and both are the eraser:
+   pushed off the bottom by a third record, or STRUCK because the board falsified it. The
+   leaving box goes absolute so neither the flow berth nor the desk's flex row reflows while
+   it rubs out — the push's FLIP target rect must be the settled one. No fill: Vue removes
+   the node at the computed duration. */
+.note-previous-leave-active,
+.note-rub-leave-active {
+  position: absolute;
+  transition: none;
+  animation:
+    ink-rub-out var(--motion-whisper) var(--ease-accelIn),
+    ink-rub-out-fade var(--motion-whisper) var(--ease-accelIn);
+}
+
+/* THE REST POSE THE KEYFRAME ENDS ON, NAMED (the house idiom: every `<Transition>` in `src/`
+   names its `-to`, `index.css:985-990`). Without it the leave class is the only statement of
+   where the ink goes, and a PRM arm that kills the animation would leave the node fully inked
+   for its whole leave. */
+.note-previous-leave-to,
+.note-rub-leave-to {
+  clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
+  opacity: 0;
+}
+
+/* PRM, AT THE SITE — the shape the three shipped `<Transition>`s use (`App.vue:1141-1153`,
+   `GameGallery.vue:1471-1494`), which is why `index.css:1157-1171` is not edited for this
+   family: line two has no write-in to name there, and the exit is a rule on the leave class
+   that a global selector list cannot reach without naming a class that only exists mid-leave.
+   `-leave-to` above then IS the reduce pose, landed on the first frame. */
+@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
+  .note-previous-leave-active,
+  .note-rub-leave-active {
+    animation: none;
+  }
