+/* ── T9-W7 §7 · LINE TWO, AND THE ONE LAW WITH TWO BERTHS ──────────────────────────────────
+   The law: the displaced line sits out of the page's flow, one rung down, after line one in
+   reading order, and it takes the air the pose already has. It never writes itself in (an
+   older line does not re-write), never takes a tone class (a refusal that has aged is a
+   record, and records are pencil), and never reaches the rule rung — 3.51:1 light / 4.37:1
+   dark is sub-AA for text in BOTH themes, which is why there is no line three and no count.
+
+   THE TIER IS THE ROW CAPTION'S, declaration for declaration: `GameControlPanel.vue`'s
+   `.zone-row-label` is the rule this mirrors. Pass 2 read the tier off the TALLY beside it —
+   but the tally is debug-gated (`GameBoard.vue:915-916`, `useDebug.ts:14`) and wants a completed
+   solve, so production never paints it and the tier was justified by a rule no visitor sees. The
+   row caption ships on every board, and it differs in exactly one declaration that matters:
+   leading 1.1, not 1.3. That is worth 2.79px of berth to a line the family was short in — an
+   18.19px box becomes 15.40 — and it is the reason the clip below had to change shape.
+
+   Pass 1's BODY size bought two defects: the longest record left 4.99% of the strip at 360, and
+   the desk pair read as one run-on sentence. The size step answers both. Selectable, because a
+   line someone leans in to read is one they may copy.
+
+   THE CLIP IS HORIZONTAL ONLY. `overflow: hidden` (pass 2) is a box on all four sides, and at
+   leading 1.3 it already sliced 0.406px off every descender; at 1.1 the rendered descender
+   reaches 2.168px below the line box and `hidden` would take all of it — a `g` with no tail, on
+   a hand face, which is a defect you see before you read. `overflow-x: clip` bounds the run
+   without establishing a scroll container, `overflow-y: visible` lets the ink out of the bottom,
+   and `text-overflow` still elides (L15 measures whether webkit agrees; if it does not, the
+   declared fallback is `overflow: hidden` + `padding-bottom: .2em` / `margin-bottom: -.2em`,
+   chosen by that reading, never by eye). */
+.margin-note-previous {
+  margin: 0;
+  width: fit-content;
+  max-width: 100%;
+  /* The row caption's five, `.zone-row-label` — but read through the BOARD estate's own role.
+     Pass 3 wrote `--type-tag`; `typography.css:123` scopes that role to the CONTROLS estate
+     (row caption · closed-tab value · roster) and R6 law 27 keeps the role set closed, so the
+     margin strip — the board's, not the controls' — was consuming outside its scope. The chair
+     ruled it (pass4/CHAIR-RULINGS §1.3 act 2): the strip reads `--type-caption`, the rung the
+     tag itself is an alias of (`--type-tag: var(--type-caption)`, byte-identical today, so not
+     one pixel moves and the seam is honoured). A `--type-margin` role, if §7 ever needs its own
+     rung, is the leader's proposal in law 27's form and the chair's row to write. */
+  font-family: var(--font-hand);
+  font-size: var(--type-caption);
+  line-height: 1.1;
+  letter-spacing: var(--type-tracking-wide);
+  color: var(--ink-press-quiet);
+  overflow-x: clip;
+  overflow-y: visible;
+  text-overflow: ellipsis;
+  white-space: nowrap;
+  pointer-events: auto;
